window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("python-for-ai", {"topics":[{"key":"python-for-ai/type-hints","sources":["van Rossum, Lehtosalo, Langa, 2014 — PEP 484: Type Hints","Official docs: typing module","Official docs: mypy"],"why":"Python is dynamically typed: a name can hold anything, and a function discovers what it was given\nonly when it tries to use it. That is fine for a fifty-line script. It falls apart in exactly the\ncode AI engineers write all day: pipelines that pass dictionaries of dictionaries from a provider\nSDK through five helper functions into a prompt template, where the first symptom of a wrong shape\nis `KeyError: 'content'` deep inside a retry loop, or a `TypeError` about concatenating `str` and\n`NoneType` two hours into an evaluation run.\n\nThe naive alternative is documentation and discipline: write the expected shape in a docstring and\ntrust callers. Docstrings drift, nobody checks them, and a refactor that renames a field silently\nbreaks every caller. The second alternative, runtime assertions everywhere, catches the error but\nonly on the code path that executes, and it clutters every function with checks that belong to the\ncontract, not the logic.\n\nType hints solve this by making the contract machine-readable. A static checker walks the whole\ncall graph without running anything and reports every place where a `Message | None` flows into a\nparameter typed `Message`. The same annotations let libraries do runtime work for free: Pydantic\nbuilds validators from them, FastAPI builds request parsers and OpenAPI schemas, and the OpenAI and\nAnthropic SDKs ship fully typed so your editor knows that `response.choices[0].message.content` can\nbe `None`. One source of truth serves the checker, the runtime and the human reader.","how":"### What the interpreter does with an annotation\nAn annotation is an expression written after a colon. For a function, `def f(x: int) -> str` makes\nPython evaluate `int` and `str` at definition time and store them in `f.__annotations__` as a dict\n`{\"x\": int, \"return\": str}`. For a class or module, `name: list[str] = []` stores the type in\n`__annotations__` of that scope. Nothing else happens. Calling `f(\"hello\")` runs normally, because\nCPython performs no checking; the annotation is metadata. This design choice is deliberate: the\nlanguage keeps zero runtime cost and full backward compatibility, and leaves enforcement to tools.\n\nBecause annotations are evaluated at definition time, a forward reference to a class defined later\nin the module fails with `NameError` unless it is quoted (`\"Message\"`) or the module opts into\npostponed evaluation with `from __future__ import annotations`, which stores every annotation as a\nstring. Libraries that need the real type then call `typing.get_type_hints(obj)` to resolve the\nstrings. Newer Python versions move toward lazy evaluation of annotations by default; the exact\nmechanics depend on the interpreter version, so check which one your project runs when a library\ncomplains about unresolved forward references.\n\n### What a checker does with it\nA static checker builds a model of every expression's type from the annotations plus inference. The\ngradual-typing rule is the key design choice: any unannotated value has type `Any`, and `Any` is\ncompatible with everything in both directions. That is *because* the authors needed typing to be\nadoptable one function at a time in a huge untyped ecosystem; a checker that refused to run on\npartially typed code would never have been adopted. The cost is that `Any` is a hole: once a value\nis `Any`, every downstream error is invisible. Most \"mypy said it was fine\" surprises trace back to\nan `Any` that entered from `json.loads`, an untyped library or a missing annotation.\n\nThe important vocabulary:\n\n- `X | None` (older spelling `Optional[X]`) says the value may be absent; the checker forces you to\n  narrow it with `if x is not None` before using it. Provider SDK responses are full of these.\n- Built-in generics `list[str]`, `dict[str, float]`, `tuple[int, ...]` describe containers. Prefer\n  abstract inputs (`Sequence[str]`, `Mapping[str, float]`, `Iterable[str]`) and concrete outputs\n  (`list[str]`) so callers can pass tuples or generators.\n- `Callable[[str, int], bool]` types a function value; `Awaitable[T]` and `Coroutine` type async\n  results, which matters when wrapping an async client.\n- `Any` means \"opt out\"; `object` means \"anything, but you may not call methods on it\". Choosing\n  `object` over `Any` for genuinely unknown inputs keeps the hole small.\n- `Annotated[T, meta]` attaches extra data to a type without changing it; Pydantic's `Field` and\n  FastAPI's `Depends` use this so the same annotation carries both the static type and runtime\n  constraints.\n- `TYPE_CHECKING` lets you import a type only for the checker, breaking import cycles and avoiding\n  loading heavy packages (torch, transformers) just for an annotation.\n- `cast(T, value)` tells the checker \"trust me\" with no runtime effect; `reveal_type(x)` prints what\n  the checker thinks `x` is, which is the fastest way to debug an inference surprise.\n\n### Why runtime libraries read them\nPydantic, dataclasses, FastAPI, `attrs` and the `instructor` library all introspect\n`__annotations__` at class creation. A `BaseModel` with `temperature: float = 0.7` becomes a\nvalidator that coerces `\"0.7\"` to `0.7` and rejects `\"hot\"`. This is the bridge between static and\nruntime typing: the annotation you wrote for the checker becomes the schema that validates a model's\nJSON output and the JSON Schema you hand to a tool-calling API. Writing the type once and getting\nall three behaviours is the main practical payoff in AI work.","example":"A thin typed wrapper around a chat completion call. The checker will flag any caller that forgets\n`content` can be `None`, and `Annotated` carries a constraint Pydantic enforces at runtime.\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom collections.abc import Sequence\nfrom typing import Annotated, Any, Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nRole = Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\"]\n\nclass Message(BaseModel):\n    role: Role\n    content: str\n\nclass GenConfig(BaseModel):\n    model: str\n    temperature: Annotated[float, Field(ge=0.0, le=2.0)] = 0.7\n    max_tokens: Annotated[int, Field(gt=0)] = 512\n\ndef to_wire(messages: Sequence[Message]) -> list[dict[str, str]]:\n    # Sequence accepts list or tuple; the return is a concrete list for the SDK.\n    return [m.model_dump() for m in messages]\n\ndef first_text(response: Any) -> str | None:\n    # SDK responses are typed in the real SDK; Any here stands in for \"unknown JSON\".\n    choice = response[\"choices\"][0]\n    return choice[\"message\"].get(\"content\")   # may be None for tool-call turns\n\ndef reply(messages: Sequence[Message], cfg: GenConfig, response: Any) -> str:\n    text = first_text(response)\n    if text is None:                 # the checker forces this narrowing\n        raise ValueError(\"model returned no text content\")\n    return text.strip()\n\ncfg = GenConfig(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", temperature=\"0.2\")   # coerced to 0.2 by Pydantic\nprint(cfg.temperature, type(cfg.temperature))            # 0.2 <class 'float'>\n# GenConfig(model=\"x\", temperature=3.0) raises a ValidationError: le=2.0\n```\n\nRun `mypy` on this file and delete the `if text is None` branch: the checker reports that\n`str | None` has no attribute `strip`. That is the whole value proposition, delivered before the\nfirst API call.","tradeoffs":["You gain compile-time detection of shape bugs across the call graph, but you pay the annotation effort up front; the payoff flips negative only for throwaway notebooks under a few hundred lines.","Gradual typing lets you adopt incrementally, but every `Any` leak silently disables checking downstream, so a codebase that is \"typed\" at 60% often catches far fewer bugs than it looks.","Annotations double as runtime schemas for Pydantic and FastAPI, saving duplicated validation code, but it couples your static types to a library's interpretation of them (for example, how a `float` field coerces strings), which can surprise you when the library changes major version.","Precise types on third-party SDKs make the editor useful and catch `None` cases, yet the SDKs' deeply nested union types can make simple code verbose; wrapping them in your own small models usually pays for itself once the same response shape is consumed in more than one place."],"pitfalls":["Annotating a parameter as `list[str]` when callers pass tuples or generators; the symptom is a wall of checker errors at call sites, fixed by accepting `Sequence[str]` or `Iterable[str]`.","Treating annotations as enforcement; the symptom is a function typed `-> int` that happily returns a string at runtime because nothing checked it, discovered much later in a `TypeError`.","Letting `json.loads` output (typed `Any`) flow through the pipeline untouched; the symptom is a clean mypy run followed by `KeyError` in production, because `Any` suppressed every downstream error. Validate at the boundary with Pydantic or a `TypedDict` so the type becomes concrete.","Forward-reference `NameError` at import time when one class annotates a field with another class defined below it; fix by quoting the name or using `from __future__ import annotations`, and know that some runtime libraries then need `get_type_hints` to resolve the strings.","Using `Optional[X]` without a default and expecting it to mean \"optional argument\"; `Optional` only means \"may be None\", and the checker will still require the argument be passed."],"when":"Annotate every function that crosses a boundary: public module functions, anything that takes or\nreturns provider SDK objects, tool handlers an agent calls, and data structures that get serialized.\nInside a tight private helper, inference is usually enough. Run a checker in CI once annotations\nexist; hints without a checker are documentation that rots. Measure the `Any` surface: mypy's\n`--strict` flag or `warn_return_any` shows where typing is leaking. In day-to-day AI work, type hints\nare what let you refactor a prompt-assembly pipeline with confidence and what make Pydantic models\nand tool schemas possible at all. In interviews, the expected depth is knowing that annotations are\ninert at runtime, how gradual typing and `Any` interact, and how Pydantic turns an annotation into a\nvalidator and a JSON Schema.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes typed wrappers around SDK calls and tool handlers so mypy catches `None` content and wrong field names before an eval run burns tokens.","architect":"Sets the policy: which boundaries must be fully typed, whether CI blocks on the checker, and that shared message and tool schemas live in one typed package.","fde":"Reads a customer's untyped integration code and adds annotations at the seams first, turning vague \"it sometimes breaks\" reports into concrete checker errors within a day."},"check":{"q":"A colleague says \"we added type hints everywhere and mypy passes, but we still get\n`KeyError` and `AttributeError: 'NoneType'` in production from our LLM pipeline\". What is the most\nlikely cause and how would you fix it?","a":"Passing mypy with those runtime errors almost always means `Any` is leaking. The provider\nresponse or `json.loads` output enters as `Any`, so every attribute access and key lookup\ndownstream is unchecked; the pipeline is \"typed\" syntactically but not semantically. The fix is to\nmake the boundary concrete: parse the raw response into a Pydantic model or a `TypedDict`\nimmediately, with `content: str | None` where the API can omit it, then let the checker force\nnarrowing. Enable `warn_return_any` and `disallow_any_generics` to find remaining holes, and check\nthat third-party stubs are installed so SDK calls are not silently `Any` either."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does CPython do with the annotation in `def f(x: int) -> str` when `f(\"a\")` is called?","options":["Raises TypeError because \"a\" is not an int","Coerces \"a\" to int if possible, otherwise raises","Nothing; the annotation is stored in `f.__annotations__` and never checked","Emits a RuntimeWarning about a type mismatch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Annotations are inert metadata evaluated at definition time. Enforcement is the job of static checkers or libraries that explicitly read the annotations, never the interpreter itself."},{"q":"Why can a fully \"type-hinted\" codebase still let shape bugs through mypy?","options":["mypy only checks functions that have docstrings","Values typed `Any` (from `json.loads`, untyped libraries or missing stubs) disable checking downstream","mypy ignores `Optional` types by default","Type hints are only checked inside classes, not module-level functions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gradual typing makes `Any` compatible with everything, so once a value is `Any` every operation on it is accepted. Untyped JSON and missing stubs are the usual sources."},{"q":"Which annotation best fits a parameter that will only be iterated over, so callers may pass a list, tuple or generator?","options":["`list[str]`","`Any`","`Iterable[str]`","`dict[str, str]`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`Iterable[str]` is the least demanding contract that still guarantees iteration, so all those callers type-check. `list[str]` rejects tuples and generators; `Any` discards checking entirely."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","sources":["Levkivskyi, Lehtosalo, Langa, 2017 — PEP 544: Protocols: Structural subtyping (static duck typing)","Traut, 2022 — PEP 695: Type Parameter Syntax","Official docs: typing module","Official docs: mypy (Protocols and structural subtyping; Generics)"],"why":"AI applications are mostly adapters around interchangeable backends: three LLM providers, two embedding\nmodels, a vector store you will replace next quarter, and a fake of each for tests. The application\ncore should say \"give me something I can call `embed(texts)` on\" and not care who implements it.\n\nThe nominal answer is an abstract base class: `class Embedder(ABC)` with an `@abstractmethod`, and\nevery implementation subclasses it. That fails in exactly the cases that matter. You cannot make a\nthird-party class (an SDK client, a `SentenceTransformer`, a customer's in-house gateway wrapper)\ninherit from your ABC without writing a wrapper whose only purpose is to inherit. Test doubles must\nimport the production base, which drags production dependencies into the test suite. And a function\nthat is happy with \"anything that has `.embed`\" is forced to demand a specific ancestry it never used.\n\nPlain duck typing is the other extreme: accept anything, find out at runtime. That is how Python\nworked for twenty years and it is why \"`AttributeError: 'OpenAI' object has no attribute 'embed'`\"\nappears forty minutes into a batch job rather than in the editor. Protocols keep duck typing's\nflexibility and add the checker's proof: the shape is written down once, and every call site is\nverified against it before anything runs.\n\nGenerics solve a different hole. Without them, a `VectorIndex` that stores \"some payload\" has to type\nit as `Any` (or `object`), and the moment a payload comes back out the checker has forgotten what it\nwas. A generic `VectorIndex[Document]` remembers, so `index.nearest(q).title` is checked, and\n`VectorIndex[dict[str, str]]` is a different, equally checked, instantiation of the same code.","how":"### Nominal versus structural\nNominal typing asks \"is this class, or one of its ancestors, the named type?\" That is what\n`isinstance` and ABCs do. Structural typing asks \"does this object have the members the type\nrequires?\" PEP 544 added structural typing to the static checkers *because* that is what idiomatic\nPython code already assumed; the checker now verifies the assumption instead of trusting it.\n\n### Defining a Protocol\nWrite a class that inherits from `typing.Protocol` and list the members with their signatures; bodies\nare `...`. Members can be methods, plain attributes (`dim: int`) or properties. A class satisfies the\nprotocol when it has every member with a compatible signature: parameter types may be wider\n(contravariant) and return types may be narrower (covariant), the same rule as overriding a method.\nThe implementing class never names the protocol. It *may* inherit from it explicitly to get default\nmethod bodies and an immediate error if a member is missing, which is a reasonable choice for classes\nyou own and impossible for classes you do not.\n\n`@runtime_checkable` lets `isinstance(obj, Embedder)` work, but the runtime check only confirms that\nattributes with the right *names* exist; it cannot inspect signatures or types. Treat it as a cheap\nsanity check at a plugin boundary, not as validation. Protocols with data members cannot be used with\n`issubclass` at all.\n\nA protocol whose only member is `__call__` types a callback: `class ToolFn(Protocol): def\n__call__(self, *, query: str) -> str: ...` accepts any function or callable object with that exact\nkeyword signature, which is more precise than `Callable[..., str]`.\n\n### Generics\nA `TypeVar` is a placeholder that the checker solves at each use. `def first(xs: Sequence[T]) -> T`\nmeans \"whatever element type you pass, that is what you get back\". A class that subclasses\n`Generic[T]` gets a type parameter that its methods can reference, and `Index[Document]` binds it.\nPython 3.12's PEP 695 syntax (`class Index[T]:`, `def first[T](xs: Sequence[T]) -> T:`) is sugar for\nthe same thing and scopes the variable to the definition. A bound (`T: Hashable`, or\n`TypeVar(\"T\", bound=Hashable)`) says what operations the body may rely on; constraints\n(`TypeVar(\"S\", str, bytes)`) restrict it to a closed list. Protocols can be generic too:\n`class Retriever(Protocol[T])` with `def search(self, q: str) -> list[T]: ...`.\n\n### Variance, the part that bites\n`list[Child]` is **not** accepted where `list[Parent]` is expected, even though `Child` subclasses\n`Parent`. The reason is that a `list` can be written to: the callee could append a different `Parent`\nsubclass into what the caller still believes is a `list[Child]`. Mutable containers are therefore\n*invariant*. Read-only views are *covariant*: `Sequence[Child]` is a `Sequence[Parent]`, because\nnothing can be put in. The practical rule: accept `Sequence`, `Mapping` or `Iterable` in parameters\nand return the concrete `list` or `dict`. Callables are contravariant in their arguments for the\nmirror-image reason.\n\n### Where the runtime stands\nNone of this runs. `Index[Document]` evaluates to a `_GenericAlias` object that constructs plain\n`Index` instances; `T` is never substituted at runtime. The guarantees come from running mypy or\npyright in CI, which is why those tools sit next to this lesson in the module.","example":"A structural `Embedder` protocol, a generic index parameterised by payload type, and a fake that\nsatisfies the protocol without inheriting. The checker verifies the `FakeEmbedder` shape and knows\nthat `nearest` returns a `dict[str, str]`.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Generic, Protocol, Sequence, TypeVar, runtime_checkable\n\n@runtime_checkable\nclass Embedder(Protocol):\n    dim: int\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]: ...\n\nclass FakeEmbedder:                      # no base class: the shape alone satisfies Embedder\n    dim = 3\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        return [[float(len(t)), 0.0, 1.0] for t in texts]\n\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")                         # Python 3.12+: `class Index[T]:` and drop Generic[T]\n\nclass Index(Generic[T]):\n    def __init__(self, embedder: Embedder) -> None:\n        self._e = embedder\n        self._items: list[tuple[list[float], T]] = []\n\n    def add(self, text: str, payload: T) -> None:\n        self._items.append((self._e.embed([text])[0], payload))\n\n    def nearest(self, query: str) -> T:\n        q = self._e.embed([query])[0]\n        dot = lambda v: sum(a * b for a, b in zip(v, q))\n        return max(self._items, key=lambda item: dot(item[0]))[1]\n\nidx: Index[dict[str, str]] = Index(FakeEmbedder())\nidx.add(\"refund policy\", {\"doc\": \"policy.md\"})\nidx.add(\"pricing\", {\"doc\": \"pricing.md\"})\nhit = idx.nearest(\"how do refunds work\")           # checker: hit is dict[str, str]\nprint(hit[\"doc\"], isinstance(FakeEmbedder(), Embedder))   # policy.md True\n# idx.add(\"x\", 42)  -> mypy: Argument 2 has incompatible type \"int\"; expected \"dict[str, str]\"\n```","tradeoffs":["Structural interfaces decouple callers from implementations, but you lose \"find all subclasses\" as a discovery tool; when a team needs an explicit registry of implementations, explicit subclassing of the protocol (allowed) restores it.","Generics give precise element types through containers, at the cost of variance reasoning; if a container is only ever read, typing parameters as `Sequence` avoids the invariance errors entirely.","ABCs fail fast at instantiation when a method is missing; Protocols only fail under the checker, so in a codebase without mypy or pyright in CI an ABC is the safer choice.","`@runtime_checkable` adds a cheap plugin-boundary check, but it verifies names only; when inputs are untrusted, a Pydantic model or explicit validation is the right tool, not `isinstance`."],"pitfalls":["Passing `list[OpenAIMessage]` to a parameter typed `list[Message]` and getting \"incompatible type\"; the fix is `Sequence[Message]` in the signature, not a cast, because `list` is invariant.","Implementing a protocol method with different parameter *names* or a positional/keyword mismatch; mypy reports the class as not satisfying the protocol and lists the conflicting members.","Trusting `isinstance(obj, Proto)` to validate a plugin; an object with an `embed` attribute that is a string passes the check and fails at the first call.","Writing `def get(self) -> T` on a generic class that never receives a `T` in its constructor or methods; the checker infers `Index[Never]` or `Index[Any]` and the generic silently stops helping.","Forgetting that `Protocol` classes cannot be instantiated and that explicit subclasses inherit default bodies while implicit ones do not; the symptom is a `NotImplementedError`-style surprise only on the implicit path."],"when":"Reach for a Protocol whenever a function or class depends on behaviour rather than ancestry: the LLM\nclient, the embedder, the retriever, the tool executor, the cache. It is the natural port in a\nports-and-adapters design and the cheapest way to make tests use fakes. Use an ABC instead when you\nown every implementation, want runtime enforcement at construction time, and want shared concrete\ncode. Use generics when a container or pipeline stage carries a payload type that callers need back\nintact; measure success by whether mypy or pyright reports zero `Any` leaking out of those paths. In\ninterviews, being able to explain why `list` is invariant and when to prefer `Sequence` is the\ndiscriminating question.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes `LLMClient` and `Embedder` protocols, codes the app against them, and ships fakes for tests that never import a vendor SDK.","architect":"Owns the port boundaries: which capabilities are protocols, how vendor adapters map onto them, and how generics keep document and tool payload types checked across layers.","fde":"Wraps a customer's unmodifiable in-house gateway class to satisfy the product's protocol, proving compatibility with the checker before any integration test runs."},"check":{"q":"Your RAG library exposes `Retriever` as an ABC. A customer wants to plug in an internal search\nclass they cannot modify, and your own tests are slow because fakes import the production module.\nWhat would you change, and what do you give up?","a":"Convert `Retriever` to a `Protocol`, generic in the document type (`Retriever[T]` with\n`search(q) -> list[T]`), so the customer's class satisfies it by shape and test fakes need no imports.\nKeep an optional concrete base (explicitly subclassing the protocol) for implementations you own that\nwant shared helpers. Type the inputs as `Sequence`/`Mapping` to avoid invariance errors. What you give\nup: construction-time enforcement, so a missing method is only caught by mypy or pyright, which must\nrun in CI; and `@runtime_checkable` checks at the plugin boundary verify names only, so argument\nvalidation at that boundary still needs explicit code."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `isinstance(obj, SomeProtocol)` check when the protocol is `@runtime_checkable`?","options":["That `obj`'s class explicitly inherits from the protocol","That attributes with the protocol's member names exist on `obj`, ignoring their signatures and types","That every method signature on `obj` matches the protocol exactly","That `obj` was validated by the type checker at import time"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The runtime check is structural but shallow: it looks for the member names only. Signature and type conformance exist only in the static checker, which is why `isinstance` is not a validation tool."},{"q":"Why does mypy reject passing a `list[Child]` to a parameter annotated `list[Parent]`?","options":["Because `Child` must be declared as a Protocol to be substitutable","Because mypy does not support subclass relationships inside containers","Because `list` is invariant: the callee could insert another `Parent` subtype into the caller's list","Because generic aliases are erased at runtime, so the relationship cannot be proved"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A mutable container accepted as `list[Parent]` may be written to with any `Parent`, which would corrupt the caller's `list[Child]`. Read-only `Sequence[Parent]` is covariant and accepts it."},{"q":"Which statement about a third-party class and your `Protocol` is true?","options":["The class satisfies the protocol if its members match, even though it never imports your module","The class must be registered with `Protocol.register()` before the checker accepts it","The class must subclass the protocol, otherwise the checker treats it as `Any`","The protocol must be decorated with `@runtime_checkable` for static checking to work"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Structural subtyping is the whole point of PEP 544: conformance is decided by shape, so classes you cannot modify fit without wrappers. `register()` belongs to ABCs, and `@runtime_checkable` only affects `isinstance`."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","sources":["Lehtosalo, 2019 — PEP 589: TypedDict","Lee, Levkivskyi, Lehtosalo, 2019 — PEP 586: Literal Types","Warsaw, Bendersky, Furman, 2013 — PEP 435: Adding an Enum type to the Python standard library","Official docs: typing and enum modules"],"why":"Every LLM API speaks JSON: a message is `{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"...\"}`, a tool call is a dict\nof arguments, a response format is one of a few strings. Typing all of that as `dict[str, Any]` and\n`str` is honest but useless: the checker cannot tell you that `{\"roel\": \"user\"}` is wrong, that\n`role=\"User\"` will be rejected by the provider, or that your `match` on the role forgot the new\n`\"tool\"` case added last sprint. The failures surface as HTTP 400s from the provider or, worse, as a\nsilently ignored message.\n\nThe opposite reflex, \"make everything a class\", fights the boundary. Provider SDKs accept dicts for\nrequest parameters; JSON files, queue payloads and logs are dicts; converting back and forth at every\nedge adds code and copies without adding safety where it is actually missing, which is inside your\nown functions that pass those dicts around.\n\n`TypedDict` exists so a dict can carry a static contract without ceasing to be a dict. `Literal`\nexists because many parameters are strings from a closed set and the set is the whole contract; the\nchecker can enforce membership and exhaustiveness for free. `Enum` exists for the cases where you\nneed the constants at *runtime*: validating user or model input, iterating all options, attaching\nbehaviour, or guaranteeing that two modules mean the same thing by `is`. Each covers a gap the other\ntwo leave open, which is why all three are in the standard library.","how":"### TypedDict: a dict with a declared shape\n`class Message(TypedDict): role: str; content: str` declares that a `Message` is a dict with exactly\nthose keys. At runtime it is a normal `dict`; the class is only metadata, and `isinstance(x, Message)`\nraises `TypeError` because there is nothing to check against. The static checker verifies three\nthings: literal constructions `{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"hi\"}` have the required keys and no\nunknown ones, `m[\"content\"]` is a `str`, and `m[\"contnet\"]` is an error. Keys are required by default;\n`total=False` makes them all optional, and `NotRequired[str]` / `Required[str]` (Python 3.11, PEP 655)\nmark individual keys. The functional form `TypedDict(\"Params\", {\"max-tokens\": int})` handles keys that\nare not valid identifiers. TypedDicts can inherit from each other to compose shapes.\n\nTwo consequences follow from \"it is just a dict\". There is no validation: a dict that arrived from\n`json.loads` is whatever it is, and the checker will happily let you annotate it as a `Message`. For\nuntrusted input, parse with Pydantic first. And a `TypedDict` is compatible with `Mapping[str, object]`\nbut not with `dict[str, Any]`, *because* a function taking `dict[str, Any]` could insert arbitrary\nkeys and break the contract; this surprises people who pass a TypedDict to a helper typed with\n`dict`. Provider SDKs lean on this design: request parameters are typically typed as TypedDicts (so\nyou pass plain dicts) while responses are validated model objects.\n\n### Literal: the value is the type\n`Role = Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\"]` means a `Role` is one of those three strings, and the\nchecker tracks the exact value. Comparisons narrow: inside `if role == \"user\":` the checker knows the\nvalue. Combined with `match` and `typing.assert_never` (3.11), you get exhaustiveness: if a new\nliteral is added and a `match` does not handle it, the `case _: assert_never(role)` line fails type\nchecking. Literals work for `str`, `int`, `bool`, `bytes` and enum members. They cost nothing at\nruntime, which also means nothing at runtime checks them; `typing.get_args(Role)` returns the tuple of\nallowed values if you need a quick membership test on external input.\n\n### Enum: constants that exist at runtime\n`class Role(Enum): USER = \"user\"` creates one singleton member per name. Members have `.name` and\n`.value`, iterate in definition order, compare by identity, and support methods. `Role(\"user\")` looks\na member up by value and raises `ValueError` for anything else, which is the runtime validation that\n`Literal` lacks. A plain `Enum` member is *not* a string: `Role.USER == \"user\"` is `False` and\n`json.dumps({\"role\": Role.USER})` raises `TypeError`. `StrEnum` (3.11) and `IntEnum` subclass `str`\nand `int`, so members compare equal to and serialise as their values, which is almost always what you\nwant at a JSON boundary. `auto()` gives the lowercased member name in a `StrEnum` and increasing\nintegers in a plain `Enum`.\n\n### Choosing\nKeep a value a `TypedDict` when it must stay a dict (SDK parameters, JSON payloads) and has no\nbehaviour. Use `Literal` when the set is small, string-shaped, and static checking is enough. Use an\n`Enum` when you need runtime validation, iteration, methods, or when the same constant is passed\nbetween modules and identity matters. In structured-output schemas all three map cleanly: Pydantic\nrenders both `Literal` and `Enum` fields as JSON Schema `enum` constraints, so the model is told the\nallowed values either way.","example":"A message type that stays a dict, a role literal checked for exhaustiveness, and a `StrEnum` that\nvalidates at runtime and serialises as a plain string. Needs Python 3.11+.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom enum import StrEnum\nfrom typing import Literal, NotRequired, TypedDict, assert_never, get_args\n\nRole = Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\"]\n\nclass Message(TypedDict):\n    role: Role\n    content: str\n    name: NotRequired[str]            # optional key; the object is still a plain dict\n\nclass Format(StrEnum):\n    TEXT = \"text\"\n    JSON = \"json_object\"\n\ndef render(m: Message) -> str:\n    match m[\"role\"]:\n        case \"system\":    return f\"[sys] {m['content']}\"\n        case \"user\":      return f\"> {m['content']}\"\n        case \"assistant\": return m[\"content\"]\n        case other:       assert_never(other)   # mypy error here if Role grows a value\n\nmsgs: list[Message] = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"hi\"}]\n# msgs.append({\"role\": \"User\", \"content\": \"x\"})   # checker: \"User\" is not a Role\n# msgs.append({\"roel\": \"user\", \"content\": \"x\"})   # checker: extra key \"roel\"\n\nfmt = Format(\"json_object\")                       # runtime lookup by value\ntry:\n    Format(\"yaml\")\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(\"rejected:\", e)                         # 'yaml' is not a valid Format\n\nprint(render(msgs[0]), isinstance(msgs[0], dict))        # > hi True\nprint(json.dumps({\"format\": fmt}), fmt == \"json_object\")  # {\"format\": \"json_object\"} True\nprint(\"tool\" in get_args(Role), [f.value for f in Format])  # False ['text', 'json_object']\n```","tradeoffs":["`TypedDict` keeps zero-copy interoperability with JSON and SDKs, but gives no runtime guarantee; once data crosses a trust boundary, Pydantic validation is worth its cost.","`Literal` costs nothing and catches typos statically, yet it cannot validate values that arrive as `str` from a request; if inputs are external, an `Enum` or explicit `get_args` check is required.","A plain `Enum` gives identity and prevents accidental string comparisons, at the price of explicit `.value` at every JSON edge; `StrEnum` removes that friction but reintroduces string equality.","Exhaustive `match` with `assert_never` turns \"added a role\" into a compile-time task list across the codebase, which is a benefit when the set is yours and a maintenance burden when it is a provider's set that changes under you."],"pitfalls":["Serialising a plain `Enum` member with `json.dumps` and hitting `TypeError: Object of type Role is not JSON serializable`; switch to `StrEnum`, or dump `.value`.","Comparing `Role.USER == \"user\"` with a plain `Enum` and silently getting `False` in a branch that then never executes; the symptom is a message type that is \"never seen\" in logs.","Treating a `TypedDict` annotation as validation and getting `KeyError: 'content'` deep in a pipeline from a malformed upstream dict; parse untrusted input with Pydantic at the boundary.","Passing a `TypedDict` to a helper typed `dict[str, Any]` and getting an incompatible-type error; the helper should accept `Mapping[str, object]` or the specific TypedDict.","Using `auto()` in a plain `Enum` and discovering integers `1, 2, 3` in serialised payloads; use explicit string values or `StrEnum`, where `auto()` yields the member name.","Forgetting `NotRequired` for optional keys, so every literal construction of the dict errors with a missing key, and reaching for `total=False`, which makes *all* keys optional and loses the contract."],"when":"Default to `TypedDict` for the shapes you exchange with SDKs and JSON files, and for lightweight\ninternal records that have no behaviour; add `Literal` to any string field with a closed set of\nvalues, and make the branches that switch on it exhaustive. Promote to an `Enum` when the set needs\nto be validated at runtime, iterated (a settings UI, a CLI `--choices`), or carries behaviour such as\n\"which models support this format\". Promote to a dataclass or Pydantic model when the record needs\nmethods or validation. The measure of success is how many shape errors move from the provider's 400\nresponses into mypy's output. In interviews, the question is usually \"when would you not use an Enum\",\nand the answer is \"when the value must stay a string the SDK accepts and static checking suffices\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Types provider message dicts as `TypedDict`s, roles and formats as `Literal`s, and switches on them with exhaustive `match` blocks that fail CI when a case is missing.","architect":"Decides which vocabularies are owned enums (validated, versioned, iterable) and which are passthrough literals mirroring a provider's API that may change independently.","fde":"Maps a customer's status codes and category strings into `StrEnum`s so integration payloads validate on arrival and serialise back unchanged."},"check":{"q":"A pipeline receives JSON events with a `kind` field that your code switches on in six places.\nProduct adds a seventh kind next month. How do you type `kind` so the change is safe, and what do you\ndo at the ingestion boundary?","a":"Define `Kind` once, as a `StrEnum` if events are validated at ingestion or as a `Literal` if a\nPydantic model already validates them, and switch on it with `match` plus `assert_never` in the\ndefault case so every one of the six sites fails type checking when the seventh value is added. At\nthe boundary, parse the raw JSON into a Pydantic model whose `kind` field is that `Enum` or `Literal`,\nso an unknown value becomes a `ValidationError` with a clear location rather than a silent fall\nthrough. Keep the event itself a `TypedDict` or model, not a bare `dict[str, Any]`, so downstream\nhelpers get checked key access. The outcome is that the new kind is a CI-visible checklist instead of\na production surprise."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the three rejects an unknown value at runtime without extra code?","options":["`TypedDict`, because the class checks keys on construction","`Literal`, because the interpreter compares against the allowed constants","`Enum`, because `Role(\"tool\")` raises `ValueError` when no member has that value","All three, since they are all enforced by the typing module"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`TypedDict` and `Literal` are pure annotations the interpreter ignores. Only `Enum` members exist at runtime, so lookup by value is a real check that fails loudly on unknown input."},{"q":"Why does `json.dumps({\"role\": Role.USER})` fail for a plain `Enum` but work for a `StrEnum`?","options":["`json` only serialises enums whose values are integers","A plain `Enum` member is not a `str`, while a `StrEnum` member subclasses `str` and serialises as its value","`StrEnum` registers a JSON encoder automatically on import","Plain `Enum` members are lazily created and not yet available to the encoder"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`json` serialises `str` subclasses as strings. A plain `Enum` member is an opaque object, so the encoder raises `TypeError`; `StrEnum` members are strings, which is why they are the usual choice at JSON boundaries."},{"q":"When is a `TypedDict` the better choice over a dataclass for a record?","options":["When the value must remain a plain dict for JSON or SDK parameters and needs no methods","When the record needs runtime validation of its field types","When instances must be hashable so they can be dict keys","When fields need default values computed in `__post_init__`"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A `TypedDict` adds a static contract to a dict without changing its runtime type, which is exactly what passthrough payloads want. Validation, hashing and post-init logic are reasons to use a dataclass or Pydantic model instead."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pydantic","sources":["Official docs: Pydantic (v2)","Official docs: pydantic-core"],"why":"The boundary problem is simple to state: a function annotated `def handle(order: Order)` is only safe\nif something made sure the thing passed in really is an `Order`. Type hints are read by the checker\nand ignored by the interpreter, so `json.loads` output annotated as `Order` is a lie the moment the\nupstream changes a field name. In AI systems the upstream is often a language model, which adds\nhallucinated keys, writes `\"2\"` where an integer was asked for, and omits required fields a few\npercent of the time. \"A few percent\" across a million calls is tens of thousands of silent corruptions\nif nothing checks.\n\nThe naive alternatives each fail in a specific way. Hand-written `if \"qty\" not in d or not isinstance(\nd[\"qty\"], int)` chains grow with every field, report only the first problem, and drift from the type\nhints they duplicate. `dataclasses` give structure but accept `Order(qty=\"two\")` without complaint.\n`jsonschema` validation gives errors but no typed object afterwards, so you validate and then still\nindex into dicts. And writing a JSON Schema by hand for the model's structured output, separately from\nthe Python class that will parse the response, guarantees the two drift apart.\n\nPydantic's premise is \"parse, don't validate\": a single declaration is the schema, the parser and the\ntype. Errors are collected, not short-circuited, and come back as a structured list with the path to\neach failure, which is exactly what you need to send back to a model for a retry or to a client as a\n422. That closed loop, schema out and validated object in, is why nearly every LLM SDK and framework\nin Python is built on it.","how":"### Compile once, validate many\nWhen the class body of a `BaseModel` subclass executes, the metaclass reads the annotations, `Field()`\nmetadata and validators, and builds a *core schema*: a tree describing every field's type, constraints\nand defaults. `pydantic-core` compiles that tree into a validator and a serialiser implemented in\nRust. This happens once per class, at import time, *because* validation is the hot path and\ninterpreting the schema on every call would be too slow. Instances are then created by walking the\ncompiled validator over the input.\n\n### Validation and modes\n`Order(**data)` and `Order.model_validate(data)` validate Python objects; `Order.model_validate_json\n(raw)` parses JSON text directly inside `pydantic-core`, skipping the intermediate `json.loads`\ndicts, which is the fast path for LLM and HTTP responses. By default Pydantic runs in *lax* mode:\n`\"2\"` becomes `2` for an `int` field, `\"true\"` becomes `True`, an ISO string becomes a `datetime`.\n`ConfigDict(strict=True)` or `Field(strict=True)` turns that off so a type mismatch is an error.\nLax is the right default at a JSON boundary where everything is a string; strict is right when the\ninput is already typed and coercion would mask a bug.\n\nEvery failure is appended to a list rather than raised immediately, and the whole list is raised as\none `ValidationError`. `e.errors()` yields dicts with `loc` (the path, e.g. `(\"items\", 0, \"qty\")`),\n`type` (a stable code such as `int_parsing` or `missing`), `msg` and the offending `input`. Stable\ncodes let you count failure modes across thousands of LLM calls and spot systematic prompt problems.\n\n### Fields, constraints and validators\n`Field()` carries constraints (`ge`, `min_length`, `pattern`), defaults, aliases and a `description`;\nthe description goes into the JSON Schema, which is how it reaches the model. `Annotated[int,\nField(ge=0)]` is the same thing attached to the type. `@field_validator(\"name\")` runs custom logic\nafter (default) or before (`mode=\"before\"`) the type validation of one field; `@model_validator\n(mode=\"after\")` sees the whole object for cross-field rules. Validators raise `ValueError` and\nPydantic wraps it into the same error list. Unlike dataclasses, a mutable default such as `[]` is\nsafe because Pydantic copies defaults per instance.\n\n### Serialisation and schema\n`model_dump()` returns Python objects, `model_dump(mode=\"json\")` returns JSON-compatible ones\n(datetimes become strings), and `model_dump_json()` returns text; `exclude_none`, `by_alias` and\n`include` shape the output. `model_json_schema()` emits JSON Schema that FastAPI publishes as OpenAPI\nand that you pass as a tool's `parameters` or a structured-output format. Field descriptions,\n`Literal`s and `Enum`s become `description` and `enum` entries, so the prompt and the parser come from\none place.\n\n### Configuration that matters for LLM work\n`extra=\"forbid\"` makes unknown keys an error, which is how you detect hallucinated or misspelled\nfields that the default `extra=\"ignore\"` would silently drop. `frozen=True` makes instances immutable\nand hashable. `validate_assignment=True` re-validates on attribute set; it is off by default because\nof cost. Discriminated unions (`Field(discriminator=\"kind\")`) select the right model for polymorphic\npayloads like tool calls by a tag field instead of trying every variant. `TypeAdapter(list[Item])`\nvalidates and serialises types that are not models, such as a top-level JSON array.","example":"An extraction schema for an LLM, the schema sent with the request, and the error list that a bad\nresponse produces. The nested `\"qty\": \"2\"` is coerced in lax mode; the wrong currency and the\nhallucinated `note` key are reported together with their paths.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, Field, ValidationError, field_validator\n\nclass LineItem(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")        # hallucinated keys are errors, not silence\n    sku: str = Field(min_length=3, description=\"Catalogue SKU, e.g. 'AB-123'\")\n    qty: int = Field(ge=1, description=\"Units ordered\")\n    unit_price: float = Field(ge=0)\n\nclass Order(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\", frozen=True)\n    customer: str\n    currency: Literal[\"USD\", \"EUR\"]\n    items: list[LineItem] = Field(min_length=1)\n\n    @field_validator(\"customer\")\n    @classmethod\n    def strip_name(cls, v: str) -> str:\n        if not v.strip():\n            raise ValueError(\"customer must not be blank\")\n        return v.strip()\n\n    @property\n    def total(self) -> float:\n        return sum(i.qty * i.unit_price for i in self.items)\n\nschema = Order.model_json_schema()                   # goes into the tool / response_format request\nprint(sorted(schema[\"properties\"]), schema[\"required\"])\n\nraw = ('{\"customer\": \" ACME \", \"currency\": \"GBP\", '\n       '\"items\": [{\"sku\": \"AB-123\", \"qty\": \"2\", \"unit_price\": 9.5, \"note\": \"rush\"}]}')\ntry:\n    Order.model_validate_json(raw)\nexcept ValidationError as e:\n    for err in e.errors():\n        print(err[\"loc\"], err[\"type\"])               # ('currency',) literal_error\n                                                     # ('items', 0, 'note') extra_forbidden\ngood = Order.model_validate_json(raw.replace(\"GBP\", \"USD\").replace(', \"note\": \"rush\"', \"\"))\nprint(good.customer, good.items[0].qty, good.total)  # ACME 2 19.0   (\"2\" was coerced to 2)\n```","tradeoffs":["Validation costs CPU per object; it is negligible for request-sized payloads but wrong for hot numeric loops, where arrays should stay NumPy or tensors and be validated at the edge only.","Lax coercion makes JSON boundaries forgiving, yet it can hide a model writing strings for numbers; switch to strict mode when you want to *measure* the model's format compliance rather than tolerate it.","`extra=\"forbid\"` catches hallucinated fields in LLM output, but on third-party API responses it turns every benign new field upstream adds into an outage; use `ignore` for data you do not control.","Generating the schema from the model keeps parser and prompt in sync, but the emitted JSON Schema (nested `$defs`, `anyOf` for optionals) is not accepted verbatim by every provider's strict structured-output mode, so some flattening may be needed per provider."],"pitfalls":["Mixing v1 and v2 APIs (`.dict()`, `@validator`, `class Config`) after an upgrade; the symptom is a wall of deprecation warnings and, for `parse_obj`-style calls, outright `AttributeError`s.","Annotating `Optional[int]` and expecting it to be optional; without `= None` the field is required, and the error list shows `type: missing` for it.","Assuming assignment is validated: `order.qty = -5` succeeds silently unless `validate_assignment= True`, so an object that passed on entry can be invalid when it leaves.","Naming a field so that it collides with Pydantic's own `model_*` methods (current releases warn for names starting with `model_validate` or `model_dump`; early 2.x releases warned for the whole `model_` prefix); the symptom is a protected-namespace warning at class creation, fixed by renaming or setting `protected_namespaces`.","Calling `json.dumps(obj.model_dump())` with datetimes or enums inside and hitting `TypeError`; use `model_dump(mode=\"json\")` or `model_dump_json()`.","Building a response model from `model_validate` on a dict that was already the model's own `model_dump()` output and losing information through aliases; set `by_alias` consistently on both sides."],"when":"Use Pydantic at every trust boundary: request and response bodies, LLM structured output and tool\narguments, configuration, messages pulled from a queue, rows loaded from a file. Inside the boundary,\nplain dataclasses or TypedDicts are lighter and sufficient. Measure validation time only when payloads\nare large or per-request object counts are high; otherwise the cost is noise next to a network call.\nDay to day it shows up as the model you hand to the OpenAI or Anthropic SDK for structured output, the\nFastAPI body type, and the thing whose `ValidationError` you feed back into a retry prompt. In\ninterviews, expect \"how would you make LLM output reliable\", and the strong answer is a Pydantic model\nwith constrained fields, `extra=\"forbid\"`, and an error-driven retry loop with a bounded attempt count.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Declares the output model, passes `model_json_schema()` to the provider, parses with `model_validate_json`, and turns `e.errors()` into a retry message with a cap on attempts.","architect":"Decides where the trust boundaries are, which models are the canonical contracts shared across services, and how strict versus lax mode and `extra` policies differ per boundary.","fde":"Encodes a customer's domain rules as field constraints and validators so integration failures surface as readable error paths during a pilot instead of silent data corruption."},"check":{"q":"Three percent of your extraction calls fail to parse. Walk through how you would use Pydantic\nto drive a retry loop, and which configuration choices change what you learn from the failures.","a":"Parse with `Model.model_validate_json(text)` inside `try/except ValidationError`, then format\n`e.errors()` (each `loc`, `msg` and `input`) into a short correction message and re-prompt with the\noriginal schema, capped at two or three attempts before escalating. Keep `Field(description=...)` on\nevery field so the schema itself teaches the model. Set `extra=\"forbid\"` to catch hallucinated or\nmisspelled keys that `ignore` would hide. Choose lax mode if you want tolerance for `\"2\"` instead of\n`2`, or strict mode if you want to count those as failures and fix the prompt. Aggregate `type` codes\nper field across calls: a cluster of `literal_error` on one field means the prompt's allowed values\nare unclear, which is a prompt fix, not a retry fix. Libraries such as Instructor package this loop."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `Model.model_validate_json(text)` do that `Model(**json.loads(text))` does not?","options":["It skips validation for fields with defaults to save time","It parses and validates in one pass inside pydantic-core without first building Python dicts","It accepts JSON with trailing commas and comments","It runs validators in strict mode automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Parsing JSON directly in the Rust core avoids materialising intermediate Python objects and is the intended fast path for API and LLM responses. Mode and leniency are governed by configuration either way."},{"q":"In default (lax) mode, what happens when an `int` field receives the JSON string `\"2\"`?","options":["A `ValidationError` with type `int_type` is raised","The value is coerced to the integer `2`","The field is set to `None` and a warning is logged","The string is kept and the field's type becomes `str`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lax mode coerces numeric strings to integers because JSON boundaries routinely stringify numbers. Strict mode would reject it; a non-numeric string such as `\"two\"` fails in both with `int_parsing`."},{"q":"Why set `extra=\"forbid\"` on models that parse LLM output specifically?","options":["It makes parsing faster by skipping unknown keys","It is required for `model_json_schema()` to emit `required` lists","It turns hallucinated or misspelled keys into errors instead of silently dropping them","It forces the model to return every optional field"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The default `ignore` discards unknown keys, so a model writing `\"quantity\"` instead of `\"qty\"` would pass with the field missing or defaulted. `forbid` surfaces the discrepancy so the retry loop can fix it."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/dataclasses","sources":["Smith, 2017 — PEP 557: Data Classes","Official docs: dataclasses module"],"why":"A generation config has a model name, a temperature and a token limit. The options for carrying it\naround are all bad in a specific way. A tuple is positional, so `cfg[1]` means nothing to a reader and\nswapping two fields is invisible. A dict is readable but `cfg[\"temprature\"]` is a `KeyError` at runtime\nand the checker cannot help. A `namedtuple` is immutable and iterable, so `for x in cfg` silently\nwalks its fields and `cfg == (\"m\", 0.0, 512)` is `True` against an unrelated tuple. A hand-written\nclass is correct on the day it is written and then `__repr__` forgets the field you added, `__eq__`\ncompares three of four fields, and the constructor's parameter order no longer matches the docstring.\n\nThe underlying problem is duplication: the field list is the single fact, and a class expresses it\nfour or five times. `@dataclass` makes the annotations the only statement of that fact and derives\neverything else from them *because* generated code cannot drift. It arrived in Python 3.7 after the\n`attrs` library had demonstrated the design for years, and it deliberately stopped short of\nvalidation and serialisation: those belong to libraries like Pydantic, which in fact offers its own\n`pydantic.dataclasses.dataclass` that adds validation to the same declaration style.","how":"### What the decorator generates\nWhen `@dataclass` runs, it walks `cls.__annotations__` in definition order, turns each annotated\nname into a `Field` (default value, `default_factory`, and flags such as `init`, `repr`, `compare`,\n`hash`, `kw_only`), and then synthesises methods by building their source text and `exec`-ing it once\nat class creation. `__init__` takes the fields as parameters in order; `__repr__` prints\n`ClassName(field=value, ...)`; `__eq__` compares the tuple of fields and only against the same class.\n`order=True` adds `__lt__` and friends using the same tuple, which is why field order determines\nsort order.\n\n### Hashing rules and why\nWith the default `eq=True, frozen=False`, the decorator sets `__hash__ = None`, making instances\nunhashable. The reason is an invariant of Python's data model: objects that compare equal must hash\nequal, and a mutable object whose fields can change after insertion into a set would corrupt the\nset. `frozen=True` makes the instance immutable by overriding `__setattr__` to raise\n`FrozenInstanceError`, and then a `__hash__` over the fields is safe, so frozen dataclasses can be\ndict keys and cache keys. `unsafe_hash=True` forces a hash on a mutable class and is named that way\nfor a reason.\n\n### Defaults and the mutable-default trap\nA default is evaluated once at class creation and shared by every instance, so `meta: dict = {}`\nwould give all instances the *same* dict. The decorator refuses such defaults with\n`ValueError: mutable default ... use default_factory`, and `field(default_factory=dict)` calls the\nfactory per instance instead. Fields with defaults must come after fields without, because they map\nonto positional parameters; `kw_only=True` (3.10) lifts that restriction by making fields\nkeyword-only, which also makes inheritance with defaults workable.\n\n### Post-init, derived fields and frozen\n`__post_init__` runs after the generated `__init__` and is the place for validation and derived\nvalues. On a frozen class it must use `object.__setattr__(self, \"name\", value)` to set anything,\nbecause the normal path raises. `InitVar[T]` declares a constructor-only parameter that is passed to\n`__post_init__` and not stored. `field(init=False)` declares a stored field the constructor does not\naccept.\n\n### Helpers\n`dataclasses.replace(obj, **changes)` returns a new instance with some fields changed, which is the\nidiom for \"modify\" a frozen config. `asdict` and `astuple` convert recursively and deep-copy\nnon-dataclass values, so they are convenient for logging and slow for large nested structures.\n`fields(cls)` lists the `Field` objects for generic code. `slots=True` (3.10) generates `__slots__`,\nremoving the per-instance `__dict__`: smaller objects and faster attribute access, at the price of no\ndynamic attributes. `match_args` (on by default since 3.10) lets `case Chunk(doc_id, text):` pattern\nmatch positionally.\n\n### What it does not do\nThere is no type checking at runtime: `Chunk(doc_id=123, text=None)` is accepted. Equality, repr and\nhashing are structural conveniences; correctness of the values is still your job or Pydantic's.","example":"A frozen, slotted config used as a cache key, with `__post_init__` validation and `replace` for\nvariants, and a mutable `Chunk` whose equality ignores the retrieval score.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import FrozenInstanceError, asdict, dataclass, field, replace\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)\nclass GenConfig:\n    model: str\n    temperature: float = 0.0\n    max_tokens: int = 512\n\n    def __post_init__(self) -> None:                 # validation hook; runs after generated __init__\n        if not 0.0 <= self.temperature <= 2.0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"temperature out of range: {self.temperature}\")\n\n@dataclass\nclass Chunk:\n    doc_id: str\n    text: str\n    meta: dict[str, str] = field(default_factory=dict)   # `= {}` is rejected at class creation\n    score: float = field(default=0.0, compare=False)     # equality ignores the retrieval score\n\nbase = GenConfig(\"small-fast-model\")\nshort = replace(base, max_tokens=64)                     # new object; base is untouched\ncache: dict[GenConfig, str] = {base: \"cached answer\"}    # frozen => hashable => usable as a key\ntry:\n    base.temperature = 0.5\nexcept FrozenInstanceError as e:\n    print(\"immutable:\", e)\n\na = Chunk(\"d1\", \"refunds within 30 days\", score=0.91)\nb = Chunk(\"d1\", \"refunds within 30 days\", score=0.12)\nprint(a == b, short in cache)                            # True False\nprint(asdict(short))     # {'model': 'small-fast-model', 'temperature': 0.0, 'max_tokens': 64}\ntry:\n    GenConfig(\"x\", temperature=3.0)\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)                                             # temperature out of range: 3.0\n# {a, b}  -> TypeError: unhashable type: 'Chunk'   (eq=True without frozen sets __hash__ = None)\n```","tradeoffs":["Generated methods never drift from the field list, but they are generic: a custom `__eq__` that compares case-insensitively means writing it yourself and passing `eq=False`.","`frozen=True` buys hashability and safe sharing across threads and tasks, yet every \"change\" is a `replace` that allocates; for objects mutated in tight loops, a mutable dataclass is the right call.","`slots=True` cuts memory and speeds attribute access, which matters for millions of chunk objects, but removes dynamic attributes and `__dict__`, which some serialisers and mocking tools rely on.","Compared with Pydantic, dataclasses are lighter and have no dependencies, but they validate nothing; the moment input is untrusted the saved cost is paid back in debugging."],"pitfalls":["Writing `tags: list[str] = []` and getting `ValueError: mutable default` at import; or, with a non-container mutable default the decorator cannot detect, instances silently sharing state.","Putting instances of a default (non-frozen) dataclass into a set or dict key and hitting `TypeError: unhashable type`; either freeze it or decide explicitly what identity means.","Assuming `frozen=True` protects nested data; the `meta` dict inside a frozen chunk is still mutable, and the symptom is a \"constant\" config whose list grew.","Adding a field without a default to a subclass of a dataclass that has defaults and getting `TypeError: non-default argument follows default argument`; use `kw_only=True`.","Calling `asdict` on objects holding large arrays or tensors for logging; it deep-copies them, and the symptom is a logging line that takes seconds and doubles memory.","Treating a dataclass as validated because it has types; `GenConfig(model=None)` constructs fine and fails later inside the SDK call with an unhelpful error."],"when":"Use a dataclass for in-process value objects you construct yourself: configs, chunk and span records,\nevaluation rows, intermediate pipeline results, and anything you want a readable `repr` and sane\nequality for. Make it frozen when it is shared, cached or used as a key. Reach for Pydantic instead\nwhen the data arrives from outside (HTTP, LLM output, files), and for a `TypedDict` when the value\nmust stay a dict for an SDK. In day-to-day work the tell is a function with eight keyword arguments\nthat travel together; in interviews, the questions are why mutable defaults are rejected and why\n`eq=True` without `frozen` makes instances unhashable, both of which come down to shared state and\nthe hash/equality invariant.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Declares configs and records as dataclasses, freezes the shared ones, and uses `replace` to derive per-experiment variants without copy-paste.","architect":"Sets the convention for which types are plain dataclasses (internal) versus Pydantic models (boundaries) so teams do not validate twice or not at all.","fde":"Captures a customer's run parameters in a frozen dataclass that doubles as the cache and audit key, so identical requests are provably identical."},"check":{"q":"A teammate proposes caching LLM responses in a dict keyed by a `RequestConfig` dataclass and is\nsurprised by `TypeError: unhashable type`. Explain the error and the correct fix, including a case\nwhere the obvious fix is still wrong.","a":"With defaults (`eq=True`, not frozen) the decorator sets `__hash__` to `None`, because a\nmutable object that is equal to another today could be mutated after insertion and break the\ndict's invariant that equal keys hash equally. The fix is `@dataclass(frozen=True)`, which blocks\nattribute assignment and generates a field-based hash. It is still wrong if any field holds a mutable\nvalue such as a `list` of stop sequences: the instance then raises `TypeError` on hashing because the\nlist is unhashable, and even a tuple would not stop someone mutating a nested dict. Convert such\nfields to tuples or frozensets, and make sure every field that should not affect caching (timestamps,\ntrace ids) is declared `compare=False` so it is excluded from both equality and the hash."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `@dataclass` reject `items: list[str] = []` instead of just allowing it?","options":["Lists cannot be type-annotated as defaults in Python","The default is evaluated once and would be shared by all instances, so a per-instance factory is required","Dataclass defaults must be immutable so `__eq__` can compare them","The decorator cannot generate a `__repr__` for list defaults"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Class-body defaults are single objects. Sharing one list across instances is the classic mutable default bug, so the decorator refuses and `field(default_factory=list)` creates a fresh one each time."},{"q":"Which configuration makes dataclass instances usable as dictionary keys?","options":["`order=True`, because keys must be sortable","`slots=True`, because it removes the mutable `__dict__`","`frozen=True` (with the default `eq=True`), which generates a field-based `__hash__`","`init=False`, because keys must not be constructed with arguments"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hashability requires that equal objects stay equal, which only an immutable object guarantees. Freezing enables the generated hash; slots and ordering do not affect hashing at all."},{"q":"What does a dataclass NOT do that Pydantic's `BaseModel` does?","options":["Generate `__init__` from annotations","Support default values and `__eq__`","Validate and coerce field values at construction time","Allow frozen, immutable instances"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dataclasses trust the caller: `Chunk(doc_id=123)` is accepted. Pydantic validates against the annotations and reports structured errors, which is why it belongs at trust boundaries and dataclasses inside them."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/object-model-and-mutability","sources":["Official docs: The Python Language Reference, Data model","Official docs: copy module"],"why":"A chat helper is written as `def ask(question, history=[])`. It works in the unit test, then in\nproduction every user sees every other user's messages. A prompt template dict is `.copy()`-ed per\ntenant and the `tools` list inside it still accumulates everyone's tools. An embedding matrix is\nsliced for normalisation and the original rows change too. A tensor is updated with an in-place\n`add_` and autograd raises on the backward pass. None of these are library bugs; they are the same\nfact seen four times: Python passes and stores *references to objects*, and mutating through any\nreference is visible through all of them.\n\nThe intuition most engineers bring from C-like languages, \"a variable holds a value and assignment\ncopies it\", is wrong here and nothing in the syntax warns you. `a = b` is indistinguishable from a\ncopy until someone mutates. The naive defence, \"copy everything defensively\", costs memory and time\nthat AI workloads cannot spare (copying a gigabyte of embeddings per request) and still fails because\nshallow copies share their contents. The real defence is a correct model: know what an object is, know\nwhich names point at it, know which operations create a new object and which change the existing one,\nand prefer immutable values for anything shared. That model is also what makes concurrency with\nthreads and async tasks tractable, because immutable data cannot race.","how":"### Objects, names and identity\nEvery value is an object with an identity (`id()`, its address in CPython), a type and a value.\nNamespaces (module globals, function locals, instance `__dict__`) are mappings from names to\nobjects. `x = expr` binds the name `x` to whatever object `expr` produced; `y = x` binds a second name\nto the *same* object, so `x is y` is `True`. Nothing was copied, because the language is designed so\nthat binding is O(1) regardless of what is bound. `==` asks whether two objects have equal values;\n`is` asks whether they are the same object. Use `is` only for singletons like `None`: CPython caches\nsmall integers and interns some strings, so `is` on numbers or text gives answers that depend on\nimplementation details.\n\n### Mutable versus immutable\nImmutable objects (`int`, `float`, `str`, `bytes`, `tuple`, `frozenset`, `bool`, `None`) cannot\nchange; every operation yields a new object. `s += \"x\"` therefore *rebinds* `s` and any other name\nstill sees the old string. Mutable objects (`list`, `dict`, `set`, `bytearray`, user-defined class\ninstances, NumPy arrays, PyTorch tensors) change in place: `lst += [1]` calls `__iadd__`, which\nextends the existing list, and every alias sees the change. The same `+=` spelling does opposite\nthings, which is why the distinction has to live in your head and not in the syntax. Methods that\nmutate conventionally return `None` (`list.sort`, `dict.update`, `random.shuffle`) precisely so\nthat `x = lst.sort()` fails loudly rather than letting you believe you got a copy.\n\n### Function arguments\nPython passes object references by assignment. Inside a function, a parameter is a new local name\nbound to the caller's object. Mutating that object (`items.append`) is visible to the caller;\nrebinding the name (`items = []`) is not. Default argument values are evaluated *once*, at `def`\ntime, and stored on the function object, which is why a mutable default is shared by all calls and\n`None` with an in-body `if x is None: x = []` is the idiom.\n\n### Containers hold references\nA list of lists built with `[[0] * 3] * 3` holds three references to one inner list; changing\n`grid[0][0]` changes every row. Shallow copies (`list(x)`, `x[:]`, `dict.copy()`, `copy.copy`)\ncreate a new outer container that references the *same* inner objects. `copy.deepcopy` recurses, at\nO(size) cost, and cannot copy things like locks, sockets or SDK clients. Class attributes are shared\nby all instances: `class Agent: history = []` is one list for every agent; the per-instance version\nmust be created in `__init__`.\n\n### Hashing and equality\nDict keys and set members must be hashable, and the data model requires equal objects to hash\nequally. Mutable built-ins are unhashable for that reason; a user class is hashable by identity\nunless it defines `__eq__` without `__hash__`, in which case it becomes unhashable, which is the rule\n`@dataclass` applies. Mutating an object after using it as a key (through a custom `__hash__`) makes\nit unfindable.\n\n### Arrays and tensors\nNumPy basic slicing (`a[:, :10]`, `a[::2]`) returns a *view* sharing the buffer, so normalising the\nslice normalises the original; `a.copy()` or fancy indexing returns an independent array. PyTorch\ndistinguishes in-place methods by a trailing underscore (`t.add_()`), and autograd refuses in-place\nchanges to values it still needs for the backward pass. In both libraries in-place operations exist\nto avoid allocating gigabytes, so the trade is explicit: memory versus aliasing risk.\n\n### Lifetime\nCPython frees an object when its reference count drops to zero; cycles are collected by the garbage\ncollector later. A large tensor stays resident while any name, container, closure, cached traceback\nor notebook output history references it, which is why `del x` sometimes frees nothing.","example":"Four aliasing bugs that occur in real LLM code, each followed by the fix: a shared mutable default\nargument that leaks conversation history between callers, a shallow-copied prompt template that\nleaks tools between tenants, the `+=` difference between a list and a string, and nested-list\nmultiplication that aliases rows. Every print shows the surprising result and, where applicable, the\ncorrect version beside it.\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\ndef ask(question: str, history: list[dict] = []) -> list[dict]:   # BUG: one list for every call\n    history.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question})\n    return history\n\nprint(len(ask(\"a\")), len(ask(\"b\")))              # 1 2  -> the second caller sees the first turn\n\ndef ask_ok(question: str, history: list[dict] | None = None) -> list[dict]:\n    history = [] if history is None else history  # fresh list per call unless one is passed\n    return [*history, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n\nBASE = {\"system\": \"You are a support bot.\", \"tools\": [\"search\"]}\n\ndef per_tenant(tenant: str) -> dict:\n    cfg = BASE.copy()                            # shallow: cfg[\"tools\"] IS BASE[\"tools\"]\n    cfg[\"tools\"].append(f\"{tenant}_crm\")         # leaks into every later tenant\n    return cfg\n\nper_tenant(\"acme\"); print(BASE[\"tools\"])        # ['search', 'acme_crm']\n\ndef per_tenant_ok(tenant: str) -> dict:\n    cfg = copy.deepcopy(BASE)                    # or rebuild; or store BASE as tuples\n    cfg[\"tools\"] = [*cfg[\"tools\"], f\"{tenant}_crm\"]\n    return cfg\n\na = [1, 2]; b = a; c = a[:]\nb.append(3)\nprint(a is b, a == c, c)                        # True False [1, 2]  (c is a shallow copy)\ns = \"x\"; t = s; s += \"y\"                        # str is immutable: += rebinds s only\nprint(t)                                        # x\ngrid = [[0] * 2] * 2; grid[0][0] = 9            # both rows are the same inner list\nprint(grid, [[0] * 2 for _ in range(2)])        # [[9, 0], [9, 0]] [[0, 0], [0, 0]]\n```","tradeoffs":["Immutable values (tuples, frozen dataclasses, `MappingProxyType`) are safe to share across tenants, threads and tasks and usable as cache keys, but every change allocates; for hot loops over large state, controlled in-place mutation wins.","In-place array and tensor operations avoid copying gigabytes, at the cost of aliasing and autograd hazards; when memory is not the constraint, out-of-place operations are easier to reason about.","`deepcopy` guarantees isolation but is O(size), can be slower than reconstructing the object, and fails on unpicklable members such as clients and locks; rebuild from a factory instead.","Freezing a container is shallow: a tuple of lists still has mutable lists inside, so the protection must be applied at every level or the invariant is only cosmetic."],"pitfalls":["A mutable default argument; the symptom is state leaking between calls, and tests that pass in isolation but fail when run together.","A mutable class attribute used as per-instance state; every `Agent` shares one `history` and the symptom is cross-conversation contamination.","Shallow-copying a nested config; the symptom is tenant A's tools or headers appearing in tenant B's requests.","`x = lst.sort()` or `cfg = d.update(...)`; the symptom is `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute`, because mutating methods return `None` by convention.","Mutating a list or dict while iterating over it; lists skip elements silently, dicts raise `RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration`.","Normalising a NumPy slice or a tensor view in place and discovering the source matrix changed; the symptom is retrieval scores that drift between runs.","GPU memory that does not come back after `del`; another reference (a list, an exception traceback, a notebook `Out[]` entry) is still holding the tensor.","Using `is` to compare strings or integers; it works in tests because of interning and fails on values built at runtime."],"when":"This is not a tool you choose; it is the model you need every time a value is shared. Reach for\nimmutability by default for anything passed between layers, stored in a cache, or used by more than\none task: tuples instead of lists for fixed sequences, frozen dataclasses for configs, `None` plus\nin-body construction for defaults. Reach for deliberate mutation when memory or speed demands it,\nand contain it inside one function with an explicit docstring note. The measures are simple: no\nmutable defaults in the codebase (linters flag them), no class-level mutable attributes, copies at\nevery tenant boundary. In interviews, \"what does this print\" questions about `+=`, default\narguments and nested list multiplication are the standard probe of this model.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds per-request message lists from scratch, keeps shared prompt templates immutable, and uses views and in-place tensor ops only where the memory saving is measured.","architect":"Sets the rule that cross-tenant and cross-request state is immutable or copied at the boundary, and designs caches around hashable, frozen keys.","fde":"Diagnoses \"customer B saw customer A's context\" incidents by tracing which shared object was mutated, usually a template or default argument in a customisation layer."},"check":{"q":"A multi-tenant service builds each request's config with `cfg = DEFAULTS.copy()` then\n`cfg[\"headers\"][\"X-Tenant\"] = tenant`. Under load, requests intermittently carry the wrong tenant\nheader. Explain the mechanism and give two fixes with their costs.","a":"`dict.copy()` is shallow: the new `cfg` is a different outer dict, but `cfg[\"headers\"]` is the\nvery same inner dict object that `DEFAULTS[\"headers\"]` references, so every request writes its\ntenant into one shared dict. With concurrency, request B's write lands between A's write and A's\nHTTP call, so A sends B's header. Fix one: `copy.deepcopy(DEFAULTS)` per request, which isolates\neverything at O(size) cost per call and breaks if DEFAULTS ever holds a client or lock. Fix two,\nusually better: make DEFAULTS immutable (tuples, `MappingProxyType`, a frozen dataclass) and build the\nper-request headers as a new dict `{**DEFAULTS[\"headers\"], \"X-Tenant\": tenant}`, which costs only the\nsmall structure that actually changes and makes the sharing impossible rather than merely avoided."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `def f(x, acc=[]): acc.append(x); return acc` return on the second call `f(2)` after `f(1)`?","options":["`[2]`, because the default is re-evaluated on every call","`[1, 2]`, because the default list was created once at definition time and is shared","A `TypeError`, because mutable defaults are rejected","`[1, 2]` only if both calls happen in the same thread"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Default values are evaluated once when `def` executes and stored on the function object. Every call that omits `acc` mutates that one list, which is why `None` plus in-body creation is the idiom."},{"q":"Why does `s += \"a\"` leave other names bound to the old string unchanged, while `lst += [1]` changes every alias of the list?","options":["Strings are copied on assignment and lists are not","The `+=` operator is only defined for mutable types","`str` is immutable so `+=` rebinds the name to a new object, whereas `list.__iadd__` extends the existing object in place","Lists are stored by reference and strings by value in the interpreter"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Same spelling, different semantics: immutable types have no in-place operator, so a new object is created and only the assigned name moves. Mutable types implement in-place augmentation and all references observe it."},{"q":"After `row = emb[0]; row /= row.sum()` with a NumPy array `emb`, what happened to `emb`?","options":["Nothing; indexing returns an independent copy of the row","Its first row was modified, because basic indexing returns a view sharing the same buffer","It was converted to a float array but its values are unchanged","A `ValueError` was raised because views cannot be modified in place"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Basic slicing and indexing in NumPy return views, so an in-place operation on the view writes into the original buffer. `emb[0].copy()` or an out-of-place expression avoids the side effect."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","sources":["Official docs: Python Language Reference and Library Reference, Built-in Exceptions","Official docs: Python Tutorial, Errors and Exceptions","Katriel, Selivanov, van Rossum, 2021 — PEP 654: Exception Groups and except*"],"why":"An LLM call can fail a dozen different ways, and the correct response differs for each. A rate limit\nwants a wait and a retry. A timeout wants a retry, maybe with a smaller request. A context-length\nerror wants the input shrunk and never a plain retry. Output that fails schema validation wants a\ncorrective re-prompt. An authentication error or content-policy refusal wants no retry at all and a\nhuman. A `KeyError` in your own parsing code wants a bug fix, not a retry loop that quietly triples\nyour bill. If all of these arrive as \"an exception\", the handler can only pick one policy for all.\n\nThe naive alternatives each break. `except Exception: retry()` retries the unretryable, including\nyour own bugs, and hides them behind success-after-retry statistics. Return codes and `None`\nresults are ignored by the first caller that forgets to check, lose the stack trace, and spread\n`if result is None` through every layer. Matching on message text (`if \"rate limit\" in str(e)`)\ncouples your logic to a vendor's wording and breaks on the next SDK release. Catching the vendor's\nown exception classes everywhere couples the whole application to one provider, so switching or\nadding a fallback model means touching every call site.\n\nCustom hierarchies solve this because `except` matches by class *and all subclasses*. A tree whose\ninner nodes are handling policies (`Transient`, `Permanent`) and whose leaves carry structured data\n(`RateLimited.retry_after`, `ContextTooLong.limit`) lets each layer catch at the granularity it can act\non. Translating vendor exceptions into that tree once, at the adapter, keeps the rest of the code\nvendor-neutral.","how":"### Raising, unwinding and catching\n`raise X(...)` creates an exception instance, attaches a traceback, and unwinds the stack frame by\nframe until a `try` block with a matching `except` is found. Matching uses `isinstance`, so\n`except Transient` catches every subclass; handlers are tested in order, which is why a subclass\nhandler must appear before its base or it is unreachable. `else` runs when no exception occurred,\n`finally` always runs, and a bare `raise` inside a handler re-raises the current exception with its\noriginal traceback intact. Since Python 3.11 a `try` block costs nothing when no exception is raised;\nraising and unwinding still costs, so exceptions are for failures, not for routine control flow in\nhot loops.\n\n### The built-in tree and what `except Exception` means\nEverything derives from `BaseException`. `Exception` is the branch for errors a program may want to\nrecover from. `KeyboardInterrupt`, `SystemExit`, `GeneratorExit` and `asyncio.CancelledError` hang\ndirectly off `BaseException` *so that* `except Exception` does not swallow them: a handler that\ncatches \"any error\" must not stop Ctrl-C, interpreter shutdown or task cancellation. Catching\n`BaseException` or writing a bare `except:` breaks exactly those mechanisms.\n\n### Designing the hierarchy\nStart with one base per subsystem (`LLMError(Exception)`) so a caller can always say \"anything from\nthe LLM layer\". Under it, split by handling policy, not by source: `Transient` (safe to retry) versus\n`Permanent` (do not retry), optionally `Recoverable` (retry after changing something). Leaves carry\nthe data the handler needs as attributes: `retry_after` seconds, token counts and limits, the list of\nvalidation errors. Give leaves a clear `__str__` for logs. Keep the tree shallow; three levels is\nusually enough, and every class must correspond to a different decision somewhere, otherwise it is\nnoise.\n\n### Chaining: keep the cause\nRaising a domain exception inside an `except` block links the original automatically as\n`__context__`, and the traceback prints \"During handling of the above exception, another exception\noccurred\", which misreads as a second bug. `raise Domain(...) from e` sets `__cause__` and prints\n\"The above exception was the direct cause\", which is the honest story and keeps the vendor stack\ntrace for debugging. `from None` suppresses the context when the original is irrelevant noise.\n`e.add_note(\"request_id=...\")` (3.11) attaches context without changing the type.\n\n### Translate at the boundary, decide at the top\nWrap vendor SDK or HTTP errors into your hierarchy in the one adapter that talks to the vendor. Let\nexceptions propagate through the layers that cannot act on them; do not catch-log-reraise at every\nlevel, which produces the same stack trace five times. Decide at the layer that owns the policy: the\nretry loop catches `Transient`, the request handler maps `Permanent` to an HTTP status, the top-level\ncatches `LLMError` for the user-facing message. Keep `__init__` signatures compatible with pickling\n(pass the constructor arguments to `super().__init__`) if exceptions cross process boundaries.\n\n### Concurrent failures\nWhen several tasks fail at once (an `asyncio.TaskGroup` fanning out tool calls), there is no single\nexception to raise. `ExceptionGroup` (3.11) bundles them and `except* Transient` selects the matching\nmembers while re-raising the rest, which keeps the retry/no-retry split correct even for parallel work.","example":"A small hierarchy, translation of HTTP failures at the adapter, and a retry loop that only retries\nwhat is marked retryable. The error-body shape is illustrative; map your provider's codes the same way.\n\n```python\nimport time, httpx\n\nclass LLMError(Exception): ...\nclass Transient(LLMError): ...                      # safe to retry\nclass Permanent(LLMError): ...                      # do not retry\n\nclass RateLimited(Transient):\n    def __init__(self, retry_after: float):\n        super().__init__(retry_after)               # args == ctor args keeps it picklable\n        self.retry_after = retry_after\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        return f\"rate limited, retry after {self.retry_after:.1f}s\"\n\nclass ContextTooLong(Permanent):\n    def __init__(self, tokens: int, limit: int):\n        super().__init__(tokens, limit)\n        self.tokens, self.limit = tokens, limit\n\ndef call_provider(client: httpx.Client, prompt: str) -> str:\n    try:\n        r = client.post(\"/v1/complete\", json={\"prompt\": prompt}, timeout=30)\n        r.raise_for_status()\n    except httpx.TimeoutException as e:\n        raise Transient(\"provider timeout\") from e  # keep the cause for logs\n    except httpx.HTTPStatusError as e:\n        status, body = e.response.status_code, e.response.json()\n        if status == 429:\n            raise RateLimited(float(e.response.headers.get(\"retry-after\", 2))) from e\n        if status >= 500:\n            raise Transient(f\"provider returned {status}\") from e\n        if body.get(\"error\", {}).get(\"code\") == \"context_length_exceeded\":\n            raise ContextTooLong(body[\"error\"][\"tokens\"], body[\"error\"][\"limit\"]) from e\n        raise Permanent(f\"provider returned {status}: {body}\") from e   # 400/401/403: stop\n    return r.json()[\"text\"]\n\ndef answer(client: httpx.Client, prompt: str, attempts: int = 3) -> str:\n    for n in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            return call_provider(client, prompt)\n        except RateLimited as e:                    # subclass handler before its base\n            time.sleep(e.retry_after)\n        except Transient:\n            time.sleep(2 ** n)\n        except ContextTooLong as e:                 # recoverable: shrink, then try again\n            prompt = prompt[: len(prompt) * e.limit // (2 * e.tokens)]\n    raise Permanent(f\"gave up after {attempts} attempts\")\n```","tradeoffs":["A policy-based tree lets callers catch exactly what they can handle, but every class must map to a distinct decision; a hierarchy mirroring the vendor's twenty error codes is coupling in disguise.","Translating at the adapter keeps the application vendor-neutral, at the cost of a mapping that must be maintained as providers add codes; unmapped errors should land in `Permanent`, not vanish.","Carrying structured data on exceptions (`retry_after`, `limit`) makes handlers precise, but non-trivial constructors must stay pickle-compatible if the exception ever crosses a process pool.","Exception groups model parallel failures faithfully, but `except*` semantics (partial matching, re-raising the remainder) are harder to read than a sequential loop; use them only where fan-out genuinely happens."],"pitfalls":["`except Exception:` around the provider call with a retry; the symptom is a `KeyError` in your own parser appearing in logs five times, each with a paid request behind it.","A bare `except:` or `except BaseException:`; the symptom is Ctrl-C being ignored and async tasks that refuse to cancel, because `CancelledError` and `KeyboardInterrupt` were swallowed.","Listing `except Transient` before `except RateLimited`; the leaf handler is unreachable and the `retry_after` hint is ignored, so you hammer a rate-limited endpoint with exponential backoff instead.","Raising a domain error without `from e`; the traceback says \"during handling of the above exception, another exception occurred\", and the on-call engineer chases a phantom second bug.","Matching on `str(e)` from an SDK; it works until a minor SDK release rewords the message and every rate limit becomes a permanent failure overnight.","Logging with `logger.exception` at every layer and re-raising; the symptom is one failure producing a dozen stack traces, which trains people to ignore logs.","A custom `__init__` that takes keyword-only arguments but passes a formatted string to `super()`; the symptom is `TypeError` when a worker process tries to unpickle the exception."],"when":"Introduce a hierarchy as soon as a subsystem has more than one failure policy, which for anything\nthat calls a model or an external API is immediately. Keep it to one base plus policy branches plus\ndata-carrying leaves; add a class only when a handler would treat it differently. Translate at the\nadapter, propagate through the middle, decide at the top. Measure by counting retries per error type\nand by checking that no handler in the codebase catches `Exception` around a paid call. In\nday-to-day work this is the shape of every provider client wrapper; in interviews, the question is\n\"how do you decide what to retry\", and the answer is \"by exception class, with the mapping done once\nwhere the vendor error enters the system\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the adapter that maps SDK and HTTP errors into `Transient`/`Permanent` leaves and the retry loop that catches only `Transient`, with backoff hints read from the exception.","architect":"Owns the shared hierarchy across services so retry, fallback and circuit-breaker policies key off the same classes, and decides which failures reach the user as which status.","fde":"Extends the mapping for a customer's gateway or proxy that rewrites provider errors, so a 429 hidden inside a 502 is still classified as transient."},"check":{"q":"A colleague wraps every provider call in `except Exception as e: log(e); retry()` with three\nattempts and reports that reliability improved. What is wrong with that picture, and how would you\nrestructure it?","a":"The improvement is partly fake: transient failures were already retryable, while the policy\nalso retries permanent ones (bad API key, content refusal, context overflow) and your own bugs,\ntripling cost and latency on exactly the calls that cannot succeed, and hiding `KeyError`-style bugs\nbehind eventual success or a generic failure. The log line at that layer also duplicates whatever the\ntop level logs. Restructure with a small hierarchy under `LLMError`: `Transient` for timeouts, 429s\nand 5xx with a `retry_after` attribute, `Permanent` for 4xx auth and policy errors, and a recoverable\n`ContextTooLong` carrying the limit. Translate vendor exceptions into it once in the adapter with\n`raise ... from e`, retry only `Transient`, shrink on `ContextTooLong`, and let everything else\npropagate to a single top-level handler that logs once with the full chain and maps to a status."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `except Exception:` not catch `KeyboardInterrupt` or `asyncio.CancelledError`?","options":["Those are warnings, not exceptions, and are handled by the `warnings` module","They derive from `BaseException` directly, so a handler for recoverable errors cannot accidentally block shutdown or cancellation","They are raised in a different thread than the handler","They are only catchable with `except*` because they arrive as exception groups"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The split between `BaseException` and `Exception` is deliberate: \"any error I might recover from\" must not include interrupts and cancellations. Catching `BaseException` or using a bare `except` does swallow them and breaks Ctrl-C and task cancellation."},{"q":"What does `raise ContextTooLong(...) from e` change compared with a plain `raise ContextTooLong(...)` inside an `except` block?","options":["It discards the original exception to keep the traceback short","It converts `e` into an instance of `ContextTooLong`","It sets `__cause__` so the traceback shows the original as the direct cause instead of an unrelated error that occurred during handling","It makes the new exception retryable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both forms keep the original reachable, but implicit chaining prints \"during handling ... another exception occurred\", which reads as two bugs. Explicit `from e` records intent and keeps the vendor trace for debugging."},{"q":"Which principle should organise a custom exception tree for an LLM client?","options":["One class per vendor error code so nothing is lost","One class per module that can raise, mirroring the package layout","Branches by handling policy (retry, recover, stop) with leaves that carry the data handlers need","A flat list of classes all inheriting directly from `Exception`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Handlers catch by class, so the tree should let a retry loop say `except Transient` and a request handler say `except Permanent`. Mirroring vendor codes or modules forces every caller to know details that belong in the adapter."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/async-await","sources":["Selivanov, 2015 — PEP 492: Coroutines with async and await syntax","Selivanov, 2016 — PEP 525: Asynchronous Generators","Official docs: asyncio"],"why":"An LLM request takes on the order of hundreds of milliseconds to several seconds, during which your\nprocess does nothing but wait for bytes. A retrieval step that embeds ten chunks sequentially pays\nten round-trips back to back; a chat server with fifty active users would need fifty things waiting\nat once. Sequential code cannot do that. Threads can, and for a handful of concurrent calls they are\nfine, but each OS thread carries its own stack, every shared structure needs a lock, preemption can\ninterleave two threads anywhere, and the GIL means they never run Python code in parallel anyway.\nThread pools are a workable patch for a few dozen waits, not a model for thousands.\n\nThe idea behind `async`/`await` is cooperative multitasking: instead of the OS interrupting threads\nat arbitrary points, a coroutine announces exactly where it is willing to pause (`await`), and a\nscheduler in the same thread runs another coroutine until the awaited I/O completes. Because switches\nhappen only at those marked points, the code between two `await`s runs atomically with respect to\nother coroutines, which eliminates most of the locking that threads require. Memory per waiting\ncoroutine is a small Python object rather than a thread stack, so tens of thousands of in-flight\ncalls are routine. Streaming token output maps naturally onto the same mechanism as `async for`.\n\nThe price is that the model is only as good as its cooperation: a `time.sleep`, a synchronous\n`requests.get`, or a CPU-heavy tokenisation loop never reaches an `await`, so the loop cannot run\nanyone else, and every user's latency spikes together. Understanding that mechanism is the whole\nlesson.","how":"### Coroutine objects\nCalling an `async def` function does not run it; it returns a *coroutine object*, a suspended\ncomputation. It runs only when something drives it: `await`ing it from another coroutine, wrapping\nit in a Task, or handing it to `asyncio.run()` at the top level. A coroutine that is created and\nnever driven triggers `RuntimeWarning: coroutine ... was never awaited`, which is the single most\ncommon async bug and a sign that an `await` is missing.\n\n### What `await` does\n`await x` requires `x` to be *awaitable*: a coroutine, a Task, a Future, or any object whose\n`__await__` returns an iterator. Mechanically, coroutines are built on the generator machinery\n(PEP 492): `await` delegates to the awaitable's iterator, and when that iterator yields, the yield\npropagates up through every enclosing `await` to the event loop. The chain always bottoms out in a\nFuture owned by the loop, created by a transport when it registers interest in a socket with the\noperating system's readiness API (`epoll`, `kqueue`, IOCP). The loop then runs other ready\ncoroutines. When the socket becomes readable, the loop sets the Future's result and resumes the\nwaiting coroutine by calling `send()` on it, which continues execution right after the `await` with\nthe value in hand. Exceptions travel the same path: a failure inside an awaited call is raised at\nthe `await` expression exactly as if it were a normal function call.\n\n### Sequential versus concurrent awaiting\n`await` runs the awaited thing to completion before continuing, so `for p in prompts: await\ncomplete(p)` is strictly sequential and gains nothing. Concurrency requires scheduling several\ncoroutines as *Tasks* so the loop can interleave them: `asyncio.gather(*coros)`, a `TaskGroup`, or\n`asyncio.create_task`. The `asyncio` lesson covers those APIs; what matters here is the distinction\nbetween \"await inline\" (one at a time) and \"schedule, then await the results\" (overlapped).\n\n### `async with` and `async for`\nResources whose setup or teardown performs I/O (an HTTP client, a database session) implement\n`__aenter__`/`__aexit__` as coroutines and are used with `async with`, so opening and closing can\nyield to the loop. Sources that produce values over time (a streaming LLM response) implement\n`__anext__` and are consumed with `async for`. An `async def` containing `yield` is an *asynchronous\ngenerator* (PEP 525), the simplest way to write such a stream; each `await` inside it can pause the\nconsumer without blocking the loop.\n\n### One loop per thread, cooperative only\nThe event loop lives on one thread and runs one coroutine at a time; there is no parallelism of\nPython code. CPU-bound work must leave the loop via `await asyncio.to_thread(fn)` (a thread, useful\nwhen the work releases the GIL or is short) or a process pool. Synchronous code cannot `await`, so\nasync \"colours\" the call chain: an async endpoint needs an async HTTP client, an async DB driver and\nasync-aware libraries down to the socket, or else it is blocking in disguise.\n\n### Error and cancellation semantics in brief\nExceptions propagate through `await` normally. A Task can be cancelled, which raises\n`CancelledError` *inside* the coroutine at its current `await`; that is a `BaseException` so generic\n`except Exception` handlers do not swallow it. The dedicated cancellation lesson goes deeper.","example":"The same ten fake provider calls awaited sequentially and concurrently, a streamed response consumed\nwith `async for`, and a demonstration that calling a coroutine runs nothing. `asyncio.sleep` stands in\nfor the network wait of an `httpx.AsyncClient` request; the mechanics are identical.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\n\nasync def complete(prompt: str, latency: float = 0.3) -> str:\n    await asyncio.sleep(latency)        # stands in for `await client.post(...)`; loop runs others\n    return f\"answer to {prompt!r}\"\n\nasync def stream(prompt: str):          # async generator: an LLM token stream looks like this\n    for tok in [\"The\", \" KV\", \" cache\", \"...\"]:\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.05)\n        yield tok\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    prompts = [f\"q{i}\" for i in range(10)]\n\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    seq = [await complete(p) for p in prompts]              # await inline: one after another\n    print(f\"sequential: {time.perf_counter() - t:.2f}s\")    # ~3.0s\n\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    conc = await asyncio.gather(*(complete(p) for p in prompts))   # ten tasks, one thread\n    print(f\"concurrent: {time.perf_counter() - t:.2f}s\")    # ~0.3s\n    assert seq == list(conc)\n\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    async for tok in stream(\"q\"):\n        print(tok, end=\"\", flush=True)                      # first token at ~50ms, not ~200ms\n    print(f\"  streamed in {time.perf_counter() - t:.2f}s\")\n\n    coro = complete(\"never\")                                # creating a coroutine runs nothing\n    print(type(coro).__name__)                              # coroutine\n    coro.close()                                            # silence \"was never awaited\"\n\n    # time.sleep(1) here would stall every other coroutine on this loop for a full second\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```","tradeoffs":["One thread can hold thousands of waiting calls cheaply, but it offers no CPU parallelism; when the bottleneck is tokenisation, parsing or local inference, a process pool or threads around a GIL-releasing library are needed instead.","Switches only at `await` remove most locking, yet they make a single blocking call catastrophic for everyone; threads degrade more gracefully when one call misbehaves.","Async composes naturally with streaming and fan-out, at the cost of colouring the whole call chain; a codebase with many synchronous libraries may be cheaper to run on a thread pool.","Unbounded fan-out is one `gather` away, which is great for throughput until the provider's rate limit answers with 429s; a semaphore or bounded task group is the flip side of the same power."],"pitfalls":["Forgetting `await`: the \"result\" is a coroutine object, nothing runs, and the log shows `RuntimeWarning: coroutine 'complete' was never awaited`.","Calling `time.sleep`, `requests.get` or a synchronous SDK client inside a coroutine; the symptom is every request's latency rising in lockstep and one core at 100% while the rest idle.","Awaiting inside a `for` loop and believing it is concurrent; the symptom is total time equal to the sum of the calls rather than the maximum.","Creating tasks with `create_task` and dropping the reference; the loop keeps only weak references, so the task can be garbage collected mid-flight and its work silently vanishes.","Calling `asyncio.run()` inside Jupyter or another running loop; the symptom is `RuntimeError: asyncio.run() cannot be called from a running event loop`, and the fix is a top-level `await`.","Catching `Exception` around an `await` and continuing; cancellation is a `BaseException` and passes through, but a handler that catches `BaseException` will block cancellation and hang shutdown."],"when":"Choose async when the workload is dominated by waiting on the network: LLM and embedding calls,\nvector store queries, webhooks, streaming to clients, agent tool fan-out. Stay synchronous for\nscripts and batch jobs with little concurrency, and move CPU-heavy steps out of the loop. Measure\nwith the loop's debug mode, which logs callbacks slower than its threshold, and by comparing wall\ntime against the slowest single call: a fan-out whose total equals the sum is sequential by mistake.\nDay to day it shows up as FastAPI endpoints, `httpx.AsyncClient`, async SDK clients and `async for`\nover token streams; in interviews, expect \"what happens if I call `time.sleep` in a handler\" and\n\"why is awaiting in a loop not concurrent\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async endpoints and clients, fans out retrieval and tool calls with bounded concurrency, and streams tokens with async generators without blocking the loop.","architect":"Decides which services run on an event loop versus worker pools, sets concurrency limits per provider, and requires async-compatible drivers across the stack.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"everything got slow at once\" incident as a blocking call on the loop, usually a synchronous SDK or logging handler dropped into an async service."},"check":{"q":"An async FastAPI service calls an embedding API and then an LLM. p50 latency is fine, but under\nload p99 explodes and one CPU core sits at 100% while others idle. What is happening and how would you\nconfirm and fix it?","a":"Something on the event loop is blocking: a synchronous HTTP client, CPU-heavy work such as\ntokenising or chunking large documents, or large JSON serialisation. Because the loop is one thread,\nevery blocking stretch delays every other in-flight request, which inflates the tail far more than\nthe median; the single busy core is the loop thread. Confirm by enabling asyncio debug mode, which\nlogs slow callbacks, or by sampling the loop thread with a profiler to see non-`await` frames\ndominating. Fix by replacing sync clients with async ones, moving CPU work to `asyncio.to_thread` or\na process pool, and bounding fan-out with a semaphore so the provider is not flooded. Then re-measure\np99 against the slowest single upstream call."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when you call an `async def` function without `await`?","options":["It runs to completion synchronously and returns its value","It returns a coroutine object and executes nothing until something drives it","It starts running in a background thread immediately","It raises `TypeError` because coroutines cannot be called directly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Calling creates a suspended coroutine. Only `await`, a Task, or `asyncio.run()` runs it, which is why a forgotten `await` produces a \"never awaited\" warning rather than an error at the call site."},{"q":"Why does a single `time.sleep(1)` inside one request handler slow down every concurrent request?","options":["Because `time.sleep` acquires the GIL for the full second","Because the event loop is one thread and only switches at `await`; a blocking call never yields, so no other coroutine can run","Because `asyncio` serialises all handlers through a global lock","Because the sleeping handler holds the shared HTTP connection pool"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cooperative scheduling means a coroutine keeps the thread until it awaits. `time.sleep` is not an awaitable, so the loop is stuck; `await asyncio.sleep(1)` yields and lets others proceed."},{"q":"Inside a coroutine, how does `[await f(p) for p in ps]` differ from `await asyncio.gather(*(f(p) for p in ps))`?","options":["They are equivalent; the comprehension is just shorter","The comprehension runs the calls in parallel threads, `gather` in one thread","The comprehension awaits each call before starting the next (sequential); `gather` schedules all as tasks so their waits overlap","`gather` returns results in completion order, the comprehension in input order"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`await` inline means \"finish this before continuing\", so the comprehension sums the latencies. `gather` wraps each coroutine in a task and the loop interleaves them, so total time approaches the slowest call; results are still returned in input order."}]}]});
